.TH WHYCANT 1 "October 2026" "whycant 1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whycant \- explain why a user can or cannot access a path
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whycant
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIPATH\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
"Permission denied" says that access failed, but not where or why.
.B whycant
walks
.I PATH
one component at a time, the way the Linux kernel does, following
symbolic links, and applies the kernel's permission rules at every step.
It stops at the first step that would fail, explains the reason in plain
words, and suggests the narrowest fix.
.PP
With no
.IR PATH ,
.B whycant
checks the current directory.
Under
.BR sudo (8),
it checks for the user who ran sudo, not for root; use
.B "\-u root"
to check root itself.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user =\fIUSER\fR
Check for
.I USER
(a name or a numeric uid). The default is the caller.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-read
Check read access. This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-write
Check write access.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-exec
Check execute access; for a directory, search ("pass through") access.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-create
Check whether
.I USER
could create
.I PATH
(as with
.BR mkdir (1)
or
.BR touch (1)).
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-r ", " \-w " or " \-x .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object per
.I PATH
on its own line (JSON Lines), for scripts. Invalid UTF-8 in file names is
replaced with U+FFFD.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SH HOW ACCESS IS DECIDED
For every directory on the way the user needs search permission
.RB ( x ).
At the end they need what was asked for.
At each step the first matching rule wins:
.IP 1. 4
root may read and write anything and search any directory, but may only
execute a file that has at least one
.B x
bit;
.IP 2. 4
the owner gets only the owner bits, even if the group or other bits allow
more;
.IP 3. 4
if the file has a POSIX ACL and its group bits (the ACL mask) are not all
zero, named user entries, then the owning group and named group entries,
then "other" decide, each limited by the mask;
.IP 4. 4
otherwise a member of the file's group gets only the group bits;
.IP 5. 4
everyone else gets the other bits.
.PP
Read-only and
.B noexec
mounts are reported as blockers that no
.BR chmod (1)
can change.
.SH SAFETY
.B whycant
only reads; it never changes a file.
It refuses to suggest fixes for password, key and admin files (such as
.IR /etc/shadow ,
.IR /etc/sudoers ,
SSH keys and
.IR /root ),
for raw disks, and for files guarded by groups whose members are
effectively root (docker, lxd, libvirt, disk, kmem, sudo, wheel, admin,
shadow).
It never suggests joining a user's personal group, and never
.BR "chmod 777" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Access is allowed.
.TP
.B 1
Access is denied.
.TP
.B 2
Trouble: bad usage, an unknown user, or a path that cannot be inspected.
With several paths, the worst status wins.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B whycant \-u www\-data /var/www/html/index.html
Why can't the web server read this page?
.TP
.B whycant \-c /opt/myapp
Could I create this directory?
.TP
.B whycant \-\-json \-w notes.txt
The same answer as JSON.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Capabilities other than full root, immutable attributes, SELinux, AppArmor
and network file system rules (such as NFS root_squash) are not modelled.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR namei (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR setfacl (1),
.BR getfacl (1),
.BR acl (5),
.BR path_resolution (7)
.SH AUTHOR
Henna Shakil. Source and issues: https://github.com/Hennashakil/whycant
